WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Hey, Thoughty2 here.
 
Have you ever heard of the world’s deadliest fart?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:13.260
No? Well, let me lead you to Cameroon, celebrated&nbsp;
for its coffee, cotton and cacao. And don’t forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.200
its football team, the Indomitable Lions, who’ve&nbsp;
qualified for the World Cup 9 times – a feat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.300
unequalled by any other African team. Their best&nbsp;
performance came in 1990 when they made it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.000
the quarter-finals and were narrowly defeated&nbsp;
by the Three Lions of England in extra time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.420
England progressed to the semi-finals, only&nbsp;
to continue its venerable custom of losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.480
on penalties, but that’s a story best left for&nbsp;
another time or, more likely, another channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.420
Anyway, let’s get back to that fatal fart.&nbsp;
Cameroon has a population of 25 million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:56.700
and over 250 native languages, but in scientific&nbsp;
circles, there stirs a story so surreal it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:03.360
eternally solidified Cameroon on the world map.&nbsp;
On the 21st of August 1986, in the desolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:10.380
reaches of Lake Nyos in the North-West, there was&nbsp;
a natural disaster - or more accurately, a limnic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:18.900
eruption. This catastrophe claimed the lives of&nbsp;
around 1,800 people and 3,500 livestock, and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.180
though it was technically a volcanic eruption,&nbsp;
it wasn’t anything like the kind you and I know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:31.440
Instead of a spewing torrent of lava and smoke,&nbsp;
this eruption materialised as a giant gas cloud.
&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.800
Lake Nyos is a deep crater lake located high&nbsp;
on the side of an inactive volcano in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:45.060
Oku volcanic plain and forms part of a 1,600km&nbsp;
chain of volcanoes known as the ‘Cameroon line’.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.280
Now, if someone tried to peddle you a property&nbsp;
perched on the precipice of a volcano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:56.400
you’d probably pass it up. Still, the areas&nbsp;
around volcanoes are fantastically fertile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.520
places for farming and, therefore, densely&nbsp;
populated. Just ask the two million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.940
who now live in the vicinity of Mount Vesuvius&nbsp;
near Naples - because who cares about trifling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.400
tales such as the destruction of Pompeii and&nbsp;
Herculaneum when you can cultivate the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.280
best tomatoes in the rich volcanic soil and make&nbsp;
the most divine pizzas known to man? Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:27.480
you get the point, and sure enough, in 1986,&nbsp;
the area around Lake Nyos was home to a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:33.240
populace of people farming crops and cattle. 
 
The Bafmen, who have inhabited the area for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.280
centuries, were some of the earliest settlers.&nbsp;
As a rule, they only built their houses on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:44.340
elevated ground above a lake, never below it – a&nbsp;
tradition rooted in local folklore that describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:51.900
lakes doing mysterious things like rising and&nbsp;
falling, changing locations, or even blowing up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.700
Scholars speculate these beliefs&nbsp;
were borne from bygone events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.200
and passed down through the type of&nbsp;
oral tradition that nobody records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.900
but we really probably should. If only they had,&nbsp;
perhaps thousands of lives would have been saved.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.960
You see, there was a ticking time bomb about&nbsp;
50 miles beneath the lake in the form of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.680
silent but deadly magma chamber. Gradually, the&nbsp;
magma released gases, including carbon dioxide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.520
which filled the surrounding rock&nbsp;
before seeping into the lake itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.760
This exact process is only known to occur&nbsp;
in a few lakes worldwide, but most notably:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.220
Lake Kivu in the Democratic Republic of the&nbsp;
Congo, and Lake Monoun, also in Cameroon.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.300
After travelling through the rock, the&nbsp;
gas dissolves into the water. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:45.300
lake's waters are stratified and don’t mix, a&nbsp;
considerable buildup of gas on the lower layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:52.740
particularly Carbon Dioxide, can occur. It can sit&nbsp;
stably in this state for some time, but a sudden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:59.100
mixing of the upper and lower levels results in&nbsp;
a limnic eruption. It’s like opening a bottle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:04.500
of champagne. Or, for the more lewd learners&nbsp;
out there - it’s like farting in the bath.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:11.820
This is precisely what happened at Lake Nyos.&nbsp;
However, several weeks before the calamitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.640
combustion, a chilling sight greeted the&nbsp;
locals one morning. All the fish in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:24.840
lake had perished overnight, their lifeless&nbsp;
bodies drifting on the surface. It transpired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.520
that the carbon dioxide levels had attained&nbsp;
a fatal concentration in the lake's depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:36.300
asphyxiating nearly all living creatures within.&nbsp;
Then, a few weeks later, came the explosion.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.940
And ever since, we’ve been trying to figure out&nbsp;
what had disturbed this deadly carbon dioxide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.860
cache. Perhaps it was a rock fall that mixed&nbsp;
the water layers. It could have been a minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:52.080
earthquake or even a small volcanic eruption at&nbsp;
the bottom of the lake – even though no tremors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.840
were reported by survivors. Whatever happened, a&nbsp;
tremendous amount of carbon dioxide was suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.440
released, anywhere between 100 thousand and 1.6&nbsp;
million tons, according to various estimates.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:10.080
Due to the inertia of the explosion, CO2&nbsp;
initially shot upwards at 60 miles per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.160
hour. At 9 pm on that fateful day,&nbsp;
villagers heard a rumbling noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:20.280
and many emerged from their homes to investigate.&nbsp;
They witnessed gas gushing upwards, forming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.740
cloud reaching up to 100 metres. Other survivors&nbsp;
described a white mist escaping from the lake.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:31.440
But what goes up must come down, especially&nbsp;
when it’s carbon dioxide and one-and-a-half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.700
times denser than air. Suddenly, the cloud&nbsp;
rushed back down to the lake’s surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.860
before billowing outwards - like dropping&nbsp;
marbles on a pane of glass. Within seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.000
the gas cloud spewed from the confines&nbsp;
of the lake, rushing down the valley in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.580
a wave that was fifty metres high, with&nbsp;
frightening speed. As it surged forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.260
the carbon dioxide forced air out of its path,&nbsp;
and as it made its way through the villages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:02.820
people in its way lost consciousness. Colourless&nbsp;
and odourless, the silent and invisible killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:08.640
claimed human and animal lives alike, its&nbsp;
victims collapsing before suffocating.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.000
The carbon dioxide was of a&nbsp;
strong enough concentration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.200
that it killed not only the people of&nbsp;
Nyos but many of those in the Subum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:23.280
Kam and Cha villages up to 15 miles away.&nbsp;
In the closest village, only four escaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:30.120
quickly enough to survive. Most died in their&nbsp;
sleep, but even the so-called ‘lucky’ few were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.620
permanently afflicted with breathing&nbsp;
problems, skin lesions and paralysis.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.600
There were other volcanic gases present –&nbsp;
survivors recounted the smell of rotten eggs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:44.520
a sure sign of sulphur dioxide – but&nbsp;
it was CO2 that proved most pernicious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.820
A survivor recalled the scenes the following&nbsp;
morning: no birdsong or the buzz of insects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:58.200
only a deathly silence. People and animals lay&nbsp;
where they had fallen. Some survivors took more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:03.900
than a day to awaken. Unsurprisingly, many who&nbsp;
did rouse believed it was the end of the world.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.540
Scientists from different nations were&nbsp;
mobilised, but at first, they were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.060
baffled. There were numerous theories –&nbsp;
perhaps it was a vicious virus epidemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.940
but the idea was swiftly dismissed as it&nbsp;
didn’t quite fit. The most popular view was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.620
that a dormant volcano had bubbled and spit&nbsp;
poisonous gases, killing those around Nyos.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.260
The true tale would only unfold in the ensuing&nbsp;
months and years, even though a similar tragedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:37.980
had struck the shores of nearby Lake Monoun two&nbsp;
years before, where 37 had died. But those deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.320
were recorded as unexplained as the idea of a&nbsp;
limnic eruption wasn’t yet fully understood.
&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:49.560
But now, the tragic toll of lost life at Lake&nbsp;
Nyos compelled authorities to take such events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:56.280
more seriously, and scientists toiled to uncover&nbsp;
the cause. One of their conclusions was that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:02.580
very same tragedy could strike again, so they&nbsp;
devised a plan to prevent this. The team fashioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:08.460
a rudimentary device to let the gas vent from&nbsp;
the lake’s lowest depths through lengthy tubes.&nbsp;

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.500
Initially, the water needed to be pumped&nbsp;
up through the pipe, but once operational,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:20.220
the outflow of gas from the ascending water caused&nbsp;
a self-sustaining effect that continually drew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:26.220
more water upwards. Similar to siphoning petrol&nbsp;
out of a car, *cough* not that I would know.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:32.640
In 2001, the first pipe was installed in Lake&nbsp;
Nyos, followed by two more a decade down the line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:39.780
By 2019, however, it was evident a single pipe&nbsp;
was enough to keep the carbon dioxide in check,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:44.040
and so that’s what remains to this day. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:49.500
keeping the residents forever safe&nbsp;
from giant explosive wet farts.

